---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/usb-not-recognized-cdj#article',
  headline: 'CDJ/XDJ Not Reading USB - Complete Emergency Guide',
  description: 'Your Pioneer CDJ or XDJ won\'t recognize the USB drive. The fastest checks first, the real causes, and the fix, whether you play in 45 minutes or tomorrow.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  // ARTICLE IMAGE (2026-08-04). Google's Article guidance asks for an image,
  // and without one an Article node is never a candidate for a rich result.
  // This is the article's own share card, generated from its own title by
  // scripts/gen-og.mjs, so it represents this article and nothing else.
  // mainEntityOfPage is an @id reference rather than a bare URL string so it
  // resolves to the WebPage node Base.astro emits, instead of being a URL
  // that merely looks like one.
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/usb-not-recognized-cdj#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/usb-not-recognized-cdj.png',
};
---
<Base
  title="CDJ or XDJ Not Reading USB? Emergency Guide | Save My Gig"
  description="Your Pioneer CDJ or XDJ won't recognise the USB drive. The fastest checks first, the real causes, and the fix, whether you play in 45 minutes or tomorrow."
  path="/fix/usb-not-recognized-cdj"
  crumbName="USB not recognised"
  ogImage="/og/usb-not-recognized-cdj.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line red">USB_Not_Recognised</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">CDJ NOT READING YOUR <span class="danger">USB</span>?<br />DO THIS, IN THIS ORDER</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-danger">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>Short version:</strong> most "dead" USBs at the booth are a connection, slot, or format problem, not dead files.</p>
    </div>

        {/* FIRST SECTION OPEN ON ARRIVAL (H5, 2026-08-05). The h1 above says
            "DO THIS, IN THIS ORDER" and this page used to answer it with three
            closed rows: zero instructions on screen for someone who is already
            panicking. The first row is the fastest checks, which is what
            everyone needs first, so it is server-rendered open. The rest stay
            closed, and the exclusive `name` still means opening another closes
            this one. `open` on the element, never JavaScript: it has to be in
            the initial HTML and it has to survive JS being off. */}
        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc" open>
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The 60-second checks</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p class="assumed">
      Reseating the drive, a wipe of the connector, the other slot and the
      other player are assumed done. Listed for completeness, not as advice.
    </p>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Give it 30+ seconds, big libraries mount slowly, especially on older CDJ-2000s.</li>
      <li>If nobody's playing on it: power-cycle the player and try again.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Still nothing? The real causes</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Wrong format:</strong> NTFS never works; exFAT only on newer firmware; GPT partition maps confuse most players. The universal answer is <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32</span> + <span class="mono" translate="no">MBR</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Corrupt database:</strong> player sees the drive but shows an error or empty library → see <a href="/fix/cdj-error-e-8302">E-8302</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Dying hardware:</strong> the drive doesn't mount on a computer either. Stop plugging it in, you're making recovery harder. There is a separate route for <a href="/recovery">when the drive really is dead</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-hungry drives:</strong> some SSDs and hub-style drives draw more power than a CDJ port gives. Plain quality flash drives are safer.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">If you play soon</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>With a laptop at the venue, you can usually rescue the gig: copy your essential tracks to the computer, reformat the drive FAT32/MBR, copy the tracks back, and play them from the folder view. You lose cues and sync, you keep the gig. The emergency flow below walks you through it step by step, including the no-laptop route.</p>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="usb_not_recognized" card />
  </main>
</Base>
